GeneralEmAb: semi-leptonic charged-current emission/absorption
(nu + n <-> l- + p, nubar + p <-> l+ + n, inverse neutron decay and
neutron decay emissivity, l = e or mu) from the full-kinematics 2D/4D
integrals of Guo et al. 2020, based on the GSI open source GitLab code
https://git.gsi.de/nucastro_archive/supernovamuonicrates

Directory layout
  wlSemiLeptonicOpacityModule2D.F90   FRONT END: InitOpacity_CC_2D,
                                      Opacity_CC_2D, FinalizeOpacity_CC_2D, and
                                      the stateless kernels
                                      Opacity_CC_2D_Energy_Nodes / _Momentum_Nodes
                                      / _Tail_Nodes (rules passed as arguments).
  wlSemiLeptonicNodes2D.F90           quadrature state: the method and its
                                      Gauss-Legendre rules, computed once by
                                      InitOpacity_CC_2D and copied to the device.
  wlSemiLeptonicPhysicsModule2D.F90   physics shared by all 2D methods: constants,
                                      angle-integrated |M|^2, kinematic windows,
                                      thermal cutoffs, Fermi-Dirac factors, gauleg.
  wlSemiLeptonicOpacityModule4D.F90   4D kernels (Vegas/CUBA, only with USE_CUBA=TRUE).
  IntegrationMethods/                 the 2D integration methods (see its README):
      wlSemiLeptonicIntegration2D_Energy.F90     ENERGY   = "STANDARD", GSI-like
      wlSemiLeptonicIntegration2D_Momentum.F90   MOMENTUM = DEFAULT
      wlSemiLeptonicIntegration2D_Tail.F90       TAIL (no thermal cutoff)
      wlSemiLeptonicOpacityIntegrationModule2D.F90  two-phase (GPU-oriented)
                                      driver of the MOMENTUM method
  OriginalGSIroutines/                the original GSI code, unchanged, used as
                                      reference by the unit tests (see its README).

Integration methods (all share the same physics and give the same rates
to within their quadrature errors)
  ENERGY ("standard"): linear Gauss-Legendre in the nucleon energy E2 and
      the lepton energy E3, nE x nE nodes, i.e. the layout of the original
      GSI routines (Ngrids = 50 there).
  MOMENTUM (default): integration in the nucleon momentum p2 and lepton
      momentum p3, with both domains split at their kinks/thresholds.
      nE outer nodes per segment, MAX(6,MIN(12,nE/4)) inner nodes per
      segment. Fastest at a given accuracy; recommended (muons: nE = 32,
      better than the GSI code at Ngrids = 50 everywhere and ~3x faster;
      nE = 24 for speed, ~5x faster).
  TAIL: kink/Fermi-split segments with a Gauss-Laguerre tail instead of the
      thermal cutoff Tfac; most accurate (reference for the tests), ~3-5x
      the cost of MOMENTUM at equal nE.

Calling sequence (the quadrature nodes are computed only once)
  CALL InitOpacity_CC_2D( nE [, Method_Option] )     ! host, once
  CALL Opacity_CC_2D( WhichCorrection, ReactionIndex, Enu, OpaA, &
                      T, Mul, Mun, Mup, ml, mn, mp, Un, Up )
                                                     ! host or device
  CALL FinalizeOpacity_CC_2D()
  InitOpacity_CC_2D stores the method and its rules in fixed-size module
  arrays (wlSemiLeptonicNodes2D) that are declared on the device
  (OMP DECLARE TARGET / ACC DECLARE CREATE) and updated by Init, so
  Opacity_CC_2D can be called inside offloaded loops with no extra data
  mapping. Call Init on the host BEFORE any offloaded region that uses
  Opacity_CC_2D; re-initialization (other nE or method) is allowed between
  kernels. Opacity_CC_2D returns 0 if Init was not called. Method_Option =
  iEmAb2D_Energy / iEmAb2D_Momentum / iEmAb2D_Tail selects the method at
  run time (default iEmAb2D_Default, below). The two-phase driver of the
  momentum method uses the same tables (CC2D_RowSetup(CD, iE2, RD),
  iE2 = 1..nE2_CC2D).

Switching the default method (Method_Option not given)
  make EMAB2D_METHOD=MOMENTUM   (default)
  make EMAB2D_METHOD=ENERGY     (the standard, GSI-like method)
  make EMAB2D_METHOD=TAIL
  (sets -DWEAKLIB_EMAB2D_<METHOD>, see Distributions/Build/Makefile_Defines;
  run 'make clobber' after changing it). The selected method is the integer
  parameter iEmAb2D_Default of wlSemiLeptonicOpacityModule2D. The meaning of
  nE depends on the method (see above).

Tests: Distributions/UnitTests/GeneralEmAb (see the README.md there):
comparison with the GSI code, convergence in nE, the three methods against
each other, parallel consistency, and the cheapest settings for a
physically weighted accuracy metric.

The 4D routines require the library CUBA (see README in the GitLab routines)

Work In Progress: Parallelization
Right now it parallelizes very poorly. I have not tried to use CUBA 
built-in parallel capabilities, I am not sure they would interact well 
with thornado/Flash-X. So my idea was to have CUBA run in serial, and
parallelize the loops over energies and grid points. But that also 
seems to be slow. 

Right now the way I am testing parallelization is to build CUBA with:
./configure
make

Then point CUBA_DIR to the directory containing libcuba.a
(e.g. export CUBA_DIR=$HOME/installed_packages/Cuba-4.2.2) and build with
USE_CUBA=TRUE (e.g. 'make cuba USE_CUBA=TRUE' in UnitTests/GeneralEmAb/Executables).

When you run the parallel 4D test (wlTestEmAb4DParallel), you have to also set 
export CUBACORES=0
export CUBACORESMAX=0
to force CUBA to use only one thread. This speeds up the computation significantly,
but it still scales horribly. Not sure why. I also had to declare all the variables
in the 2D and 4D modules as threadprivate, but maybe that is not the best option?
Have to investigate this more. Maybe the overhead is caused by Vegas being called 
N times on N threads? I still don't understand why the parallel can be SLOWER than
the serial...

Other doubts/questions
- In the original routines there are lots of REAL(4) numbers (i.e. 1.0 instead of 1.0d0, etc...)
If you compile with -fdefault-real-8 it should be fine, but it is still kinda weird to not
do it explicitly. So in my routines I changed all of them
- Tfac is a thermal cutoff in units of T on the Fermi-Dirac tails (150 in the GSI 2D code,
  100 in the 4D code; 30, measured from the kinematic threshold, in the 2D methods here;
  the TAIL method needs none).
- The threadprivate declaration in wlSemiLeptonicOpacityModule4D.F90 is guarded by
  '#if defined(USE_OMP)', but the build system defines WEAKLIB_OMP (not USE_OMP), so the
  module globals are currently NOT threadprivate and the OpenMP 4D loop is racy. This
  may be part of the poor/negative scaling seen in wlTestEmAb4DParallel.
- Integration_D in wlSemiLeptonicOpacityModule4D.F90 divides the reaction-4 emissivity
  by an extra 1.0d5 (it is already in (s cm^3 MeV)^-1), so 4D emissivities are 1e-5 too
  small compared to the 2D module and the GSI code (seen in wlTestEmAb4DVsGSI).
